<Window xmlns="clr-namespace:MGUI.Core.UI.XAML;assembly=MGUI.Core"
        Left="440" Top="20" MinHeight="0" Width="500" Height="800" TitleText="Window" Padding="10">
    <Window.Styles>
        <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="10" />
        </Style>
        <Style Name="Header1" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="IsBold" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14" />
        </Style>
        <Style TargetType="Separator">
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="2" />
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="White" />
            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.25" />
        </Style>
        <Style TargetType="Expander">
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4" />
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="White * 0.1" />
        </Style>
    </Window.Styles>

    <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" Background="rgb(64,64,64)" Padding="6,3">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="10">
            <TextBlock StyleNames="Header1" Text="Window:" HorizontalAlignment="Center" />
            <Separator/>
            <TextBlock>
A root window is normally placed by an absolute [b]Left[/b] and [b]Top[/b]. It can instead say where it sits inside the view, and then follow a view that changes size.
            </TextBlock>
            <Separator />

            <DockPanel>
                <TextBlock Dock="Top" StyleNames="Header1" Text="Screen placement" />
                <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Background="White * 0.1" Padding="4" Margin="0,4,0,0" Spacing="4">
                    <TextBlock>
[b]ScreenHorizontalAlignment[/b] and [b]ScreenVerticalAlignment[/b] place this window inside the view instead of at an absolute position. [b]ScreenMargin[/b] is the inset from the edge it aligns to. It is not [b]Margin[/b], which on a root window insets the window's own content instead.
                    </TextBlock>
                    <TextBlock>
An aligned window is also kept within the space that inset leaves, so it can never be larger than the view. That matters in a split view, where each viewport is only a fraction of the back buffer. A [b]MinWidth[/b] or [b]MinHeight[/b] still wins over the available space.
                    </TextBlock>
                    <TextBlock>
Move this very window, and notice it is re-placed every tick rather than once:
                    </TextBlock>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                        <Button Name="btnPlaceTopLeft"><TextBlock Text="Top left" /></Button>
                        <Button Name="btnPlaceCenter"><TextBlock Text="Centre" /></Button>
                        <Button Name="btnPlaceBottomRight"><TextBlock Text="Bottom right" /></Button>
                    </StackPanel>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                        <Button Name="btnPlaceStretch"><TextBlock Text="Stretch width" /></Button>
                        <Button Name="btnPlaceNone"><TextBlock Text="Back to absolute" /></Button>
                    </StackPanel>
                </StackPanel>
            </DockPanel>
            <Separator/>

        </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>
</Window>